{
  "id": "proj-end-grain-cutting-board",
  "slug": "end-grain-cutting-board",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "End-Grain Cutting Board",
  "summary": "A checkerboard of maple and walnut end grain. Two glue-ups and a lot of careful sanding.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 6,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Check the turning squares with the combination square. They are often not quite square; if one is off, skim two adjacent faces at the table saw so all six match.",
    "First glue-up: lay the six squares side by side, alternating maple and walnut, and glue the long edges. Alternate clamps above and below the panel, bring the faces flush, scrape off the squeeze-out once it turns rubbery, and leave it overnight.",
    "Flatten both faces of the panel with the orbit sander, then cut one end square at the table saw.",
    "Crosscut the panel into strips 1 3/4 in wide so each square comes out as a true cube, using a crosscut sled with a stop block. Never use the rip fence as a stop for a crosscut: the trapped offcut kicks back.",
    "Flip every other strip end for end to make the checkerboard. Brush a thin coat of glue on the end-grain faces, let it soak in for a few minutes, then glue and clamp with cauls keeping the strips aligned. Leave it overnight.",
    "Flatten the end-grain faces with the orbit sander from 80 grit, checking with a straightedge. Do not put an end-grain board through a thickness planer; it can tear out the far edge or throw the board.",
    "Trim the long edges square, then rout the 1/4 in roundover: do the end-grain edges first and the long-grain edges last, so the last passes clean up any corner tear-out.",
    "Sand 80 to 220 grit, wipe with a damp cloth to raise the grain, let it dry, and sand again at 220.",
    "Flood the board with mineral oil, wait, and repeat until it stops soaking in (several coats over a day). Finish with board wax and screw on the rubber feet."
  ],
